WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:05.950
Chronicling the white-knuckle thrills of crab
fishing in the harsh seas of the Bering Strait,

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:09.950
Deadliest Catch has been one of Discovery
Channel's hottest shows for more than a decade.

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:15.009
That's time enough to bury plenty of skeletons
at sea, so here's a look at some salty behind-the-scenes

00:00:15.009 --> 00:00:19.470
tales even die-hard fans may not know about
Deadliest Catch.

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:20.529
Almost deadliest

00:00:20.529 --> 00:00:25.900
With all of the carnage left in the wake of
the Deadliest Catch crab boats, it may be

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:30.460
surprising to find out that the crab seasons
followed by the show — the red king crab

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:35.580
and opilio crab — don't traditionally have
the highest body counts for fisherman.

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:40.790
According to The Oregonian, that dubious honor
goes to the Dungeness crab industry, with

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:43.330
a death rate "twice that of the Bering Sea."

00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:45.640
This is apparently due to two factors.

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:50.120
One, Dungeness boats don't need a Coast Guard
safety inspection to get permitted.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:56.320
And two, the Dungeness industry still operates
on the "derby" system, which allows for "inexperienced

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:00.690
captains and small boats" to incur high risks
trying to catch as much crab as they possibly

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:01.690
can.

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:05.680
The Discovery Channel apparently realized
the superlative flaw in the show's title,

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:10.080
which they've since corrected by launching
their spinoff series, Deadliest Catch: Dungeon

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:15.530
Cove, which documents — you guessed it — the
Dungeness crab industry.

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:16.790
Arrest warrant

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:21.980
Jake Harris, son of the late Captain Phil
Harris, has had several reported run-ins with

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:25.250
the law for drug use and driving while intoxicated.

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:29.220
While it seemed like Harris was getting a
handle on his personal issues, things started

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:31.090
going back downhill fast.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:36.320
First, Harris was attacked in an apparent
robbery while sharing a ride home from a casino,

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:38.310
according to Seattle's Channel 7 news.

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:42.659
Harris was badly beaten, suffering a "cracked
skull and brain trauma."

00:01:42.659 --> 00:01:48.140
A few months later, TMZ reported that Harris
allegedly stole a woman's car in Phoenix and

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:53.920
was subsequently busted by police for possession
of crystal meth and xanax . Harris then failed

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:59.330
to show up in court to face the charges stemming
from the arrest, and as of May 12, 2017 has

00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:03.259
a bench warrant out for his arrest, according
to Fox News.

00:02:03.259 --> 00:02:04.259
Double jeopardy

00:02:04.259 --> 00:02:10.509
In 2016, Sig Hansen's estranged daughter from
his first marriage, Melissa Eckstrom, opened

00:02:10.509 --> 00:02:15.470
a civil suit claiming that her father sexually
abused her when she was two years old.

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:20.799
The case is complicated, since Hansen was
exonerated of the same charges in 1990 during

00:02:20.799 --> 00:02:25.370
a contentious divorce and custody case in
which the judge ruled that the abuse did not

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:26.370
occur.

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:30.540
Though Hansen relinquished parental rights
to Melissa, he claims her mother has continually

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:32.349
attempted to extort him.

00:02:32.349 --> 00:02:36.799
Hansen told The Seattle Times, "This is nothing
more than an old-fashioned shakedown.

00:02:36.799 --> 00:02:41.480
It's a completely frivolous lawsuit full of
lies that my ex-wife made up to take away

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:45.239
my daughter, and still uses to try to extort
money from me.

00:02:45.239 --> 00:02:46.749
It's blackmail."

00:02:46.749 --> 00:02:48.049
Pain in the Uber

00:02:48.049 --> 00:02:54.799
Around the time the abuse allegations resurfaced,
Sig Hansen made news again for allegedly flipping

00:02:54.799 --> 00:02:56.620
out on an Uber driver.

00:02:56.620 --> 00:03:01.010
According to The Seattle Times, Hansen and
his son-in-law "spat on the driver's head

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:03.290
and the back of the driver's seat."

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:07.989
Hansen also allegedly "kicked the outside
of the rear of the passenger side of the vehicle,

00:03:07.989 --> 00:03:09.379
causing a dent."

00:03:09.379 --> 00:03:14.840
Hansen was arrested at his home after a drunken
confrontation with Seattle PD, but later apologized

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:16.639
in multiple tweets for his actions.

00:03:16.639 --> 00:03:20.579
"I am terribly sorry for my behavior and am
very embarrassed by it.

00:03:20.579 --> 00:03:25.419
I owe a bunch of people apologies, first and
foremost to our Uber driver, who was just

00:03:25.419 --> 00:03:26.739
trying to get us home safely.

00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:28.709
I hope I can make that apology in person.

00:03:28.709 --> 00:03:32.489
I have no excuse, and accept responsibility
for my actions."

00:03:32.489 --> 00:03:35.780
"I'm Captain Sig and I approve this message,
now get slammin!"

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:38.389
Murder

00:03:38.389 --> 00:03:43.269
Deadliest Catch has tragically covered the
deaths of multiple crew members who died working

00:03:43.269 --> 00:03:48.180
in violent storm conditions, but tragedy has
also touched the show far from the docks of

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:49.239
Dutch Harbor.

00:03:49.239 --> 00:03:55.280
In a seemingly random incident, associate
producer Joseph McMahon was gunned down outside

00:03:55.280 --> 00:04:00.629
his California home after he went outside
to investigate a noise, according to CBS LA.

00:04:00.629 --> 00:04:04.829
And if that wasn't bizarre enough, the suspect
in the shooting was found dead in his car

00:04:04.829 --> 00:04:10.920
that same day from "what appears to be a self-inflicted
gunshot wound," according to The Wrap.

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:12.010
Dining woes

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:17.019
With the irregular hours and backbreaking
shifts the crews have to deal with, mealtimes

00:04:17.019 --> 00:04:19.920
on a crabbing vessel are few and far between.

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:23.180
In an interview with the Miami New Times,
Sig Hansen explained:

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:26.070
"On the boat, you don't know when you're gonna
eat again.

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:28.750
There are no specified meal times.

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:33.160
Fishing is what dictates your meals and what
you can eat, so your diet is horrifying.

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:35.750
You're not eating at regular times, it's hell."

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:38.580
And one thing they're definitely not eating?

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:39.590
Crab.

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:44.120
Despite being surrounded by the eight-legged
delicacies, you'll never see the Deadliest

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:49.200
Catch fisherman sitting around a table sucking
down crab meat dunked in melted butter.

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:53.410
Crabs are the reason they're out there risking
their lives, so it would be pretty counterproductive

00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:57.520
to continually put a dent in their profits
by eating the catch.

00:04:57.520 --> 00:04:58.720
Cameraman peril

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:03.230
A lot of reality shows that feature tough
characters in gruelling environments generally

00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:07.090
provide an opportunity for the production
team to leave those elements behind at the

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:08.090
end of their shift.

00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:10.730
On Deadliest Catch, this is not the case.

00:05:10.730 --> 00:05:15.420
According to associate producer Ryan McNeil,
the two-man video crews aboard the crab boats

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:16.710
don't have it easy.

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:21.460
Not only do they endure the same weather conditions
and rough seas as the fishermen, they do it

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:25.710
all without ever leaving the boat, which means
they're stuck on deck with the crew for "the

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:29.610
entire crab run," which generally lasts "three
to five weeks."

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:34.900
This leads to an inevitable camaraderie between
the fishing and film crews, according to Captain

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:37.430
"Wild" Bill Wichrowski , who told The A.V.

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:38.430
Club:

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:42.870
"I'd say 85 percent of the [camera] guys I
had ended up being buddies...We actually spend

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:47.120
more time with the cameramen on the boat than
we do with our own families, if you think

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:48.120
about it.

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:52.920
You're with them 24/7… and you don't see
your own family that much."

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:54.290
Climate chaos

00:05:54.290 --> 00:05:58.500
Environmental factors obviously play a huge
part on Deadliest Catch.

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:03.520
Raging storms, colossal waves, and frigid
water are all hazards the crab boat crews

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:05.350
face on a daily basis.

00:06:05.350 --> 00:06:10.390
But on Season 13, the crews planned to change
tactics due to climate change.

00:06:10.390 --> 00:06:15.450
Speaking with The Daily Mail, executive producer
R. Decker Watson, Jr. said that the rise in

00:06:15.450 --> 00:06:20.220
water temperature has forced some of the boats
to "strike out for new territory that is more

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:25.420
dangerous because of fiercer storms and is
further from rescue workers if something goes

00:06:25.420 --> 00:06:26.420
wrong."

00:06:26.420 --> 00:06:29.890
Though Watson admits the show is careful to
avoid the political controversy surrounding

00:06:29.890 --> 00:06:34.310
climate change, he does admit that both the
production staff and the crabbing crews have

00:06:34.310 --> 00:06:39.900
a personal stake in how the changing environment
affects the fishing industry as a whole, saying:

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:43.170
"When something like this comes up, it's felt
by all of us.

00:06:43.170 --> 00:06:45.920
I love making this show, and so does the rest
of my team."

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:47.420
Thanks for watching!

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:50.130
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:50.130 --> 00:06:52.139
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

